  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA ridge of high pressure, which is currently in control over the \nMiddle Ohio Valley and much of the southern US, will begin to drift \nsouth as a cold front approaches mid week. Despite this retreat, dry \nweather is expected to persist through Wednesday afternoon.\n\nA subtle warming trend will continue through the middle of the week, \nwith daytime high temperatures likely peaking around 5 to 10 degrees \nabove normal each afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected to invade southeast Ohio in \nadvance of a cold front Wednesday night, then precipitation will \nbecome more widespread as the forges into the area on Thursday. \n\nBountiful moisture should facilitate heavy downpours, which, \ncombined with the potential for training storms, will raise concerns \nof localized flash flooding within the area. Best potential for \nhydro-related issues is likely to be on Thursday. \n\nAlthough model soundings show a decent amount of shear developing on \nThursday, cloud cover and early activity are anticipated to limit \ninstability. Consequently, severe potential concomitant with the \nfront should be low.\n\nThe front shifts south of the area by Friday morning, though\nlingering moisture may sustain some activity primarily in and \nnear the mountains during the day. A couple of shortwaves then \nproffer additional opportunities for showers and storms this \nweekend and early next week.",
